.TH FSEEK 3
.SH NAME
fseek, ftell, rewind \- reposition a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
fseek(stream, offset, ptrname)
FILE *stream;
long offset;
int ptrname;

long
ftell(stream)
FILE *stream;

rewind(stream)
FILE *stream;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fseek
sets the position at which the next read or write on
.I stream
takes place:
.I offset
bytes from the beginning of the file if
.I ptrname
is 0, from the current position if it is 1, or from the end of the
file if it is 2. What is waiting in the stream's buffer to be written is
written first; what was read into the buffer and not yet taken, and a
character pushed back by
.BR ungetc (3),
are forgotten. The end of file flag is cleared.
.PP
.B ftell
returns the current position of
.IR stream ,
in bytes from the beginning of the file. It is the offset to give
fseek, with ptrname 0, to come back to the same place.
.PP
.B rewind(stream)
is
.BR "fseek(stream, 0L, 0)" ,
and clears the error flag as well.
.PP
On a stream open for both reading and writing, one of these must come
between a read and a write.
.PP
The offset is a long: write 0L, not 0, or the high word of the argument
is whatever is on the stack.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.B fseek
returns 0, or \-1 if the file cannot be positioned (a pipe), or the
position would be before the beginning of the file.
.B ftell
returns the position, or \-1 after an error.
.SH SEE ALSO
lseek(2), fopen(3), ungetc(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.BR fseek ,
.B ftell
and
.B rewind
functions first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
